A||N||3||Daleford||Wheel adapters: safe or sorry?||||5||
Z||000000||Daleford||11-24-2001||11:58 AM||||Building a kitcar and need wider track.  The easy way is of course, wheel adapters.<BR>Many builders go this route, however some people so NO!  Has anyone ever heard of a wheel adapter failing? <BR>Should mention I have a complete 4.9 Caddy powertrain installed in the rear of a stretched Fiero so there will be some torque!<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-24-2001||12:33 PM||||Aftermarket wheels, wider and/or with reverse offset, are best.<P>Wheel adaptors can break. depends what you do to them. Unless something has changed they are banned on just about all race tracks because they can't be trusted. Most are cast Al peices that aren't all that strong.<P>Now a wheel spacer, used with long lug studs... that's a bit differant. The spacer doesn't get the load a adapter does. The lug loads pass straight thru a spacer just like a brake rotor's hat vs. an adaptor that's bolted to the hub and then the wheel bolted to it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||11-24-2001||02:19 PM||||NEVER run adapters. Yes I have seen them fail. It wasent pretty.<BR>The best way to get the track width right it to use a wide suspesion. Like the stuff offered by HELD MOTORSPORTS.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||11-24-2001||06:37 PM||||The safest way and best way is one of the kits that have longer control arms and axles. That way you dont even put a load on wheel hubs and bearings. I got my rear wheels with 2 inch deeper than normal offset to make them sit rite. Ive seen some 355 kits with a adapter that has to be 6 inches thick! Id never ever use any kind of adapter-period.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
